          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line treatment for newly diagnosed high blood pressure?",
          "answer": "The guidance I searched describes first-line drug treatment for most adults with high blood pressure as one of four drug classes: thiazide-type diuretics, ACE inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers, or dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers. (Source: \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line,\" a major cardiology society consensus summary.)\n\nThe same document says an ACE inhibitor and an angiotensin receptor blocker should not be combined, because that adds harm without adding benefit.\n\nThe guidance also says that for many people with newly diagnosed stage 1 high blood pressure and no other serious risk factors, lifestyle changes come first, with a reassessment after three to six months before drugs are considered. Diet and physical activity notes in the same search (from general clinical reference sources) support lifestyle measures such as reducing salt, drinking less alcohol, and losing weight, but they do not name specific drug choices.\n\nThe search did not cover which drug suits a particular person, doses, or how to choose between the four classes. A clinician who knows your full health history makes that decision. If you have chest pain, fainting, or severe breathlessness, seek urgent care right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
